% === provenance: Knuth1984 ===
% CLAIM: Knuth (1984) defined LP by two aims: explaining to human beings what
%   we want a computer to do, and presenting a program's concepts in the
%   order best for human understanding.
% FOUND-VIA: known item. Metadata from Crossref
%   (curl https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1093/comjnl/27.2.97: Comput. J.
%   27(2):97-111, 1984). Published version read on the user's reMarkable
%   (/Literate programming/Literate Programming, OUP download, rendered pages
%   1 and 11 = journal pp. 97 and 107). Author's preprint ("submitted to THE
%   COMPUTER JOURNAL", TeX output 1995) fetched with fetch_pdf.py from
%   http://www.literateprogramming.com/knuthweb.pdf. Forward chaining in
%   OpenAlex (cites:W2342082360, from 2023-01-01: 180 works) used this item
%   as the seed.
% PICKED: primary source; the definition of LP.
% QUOTE (p. 97, sec. A): "Let us change our traditional attitude to the
%   construction of programs: Instead of imagining that our main task is to
%   instruct a computer what to do, let us concentrate rather on explaining
%   to human beings what we want a computer to do."
% QUOTE (p. 97, sec. A): "He or she strives for a program that is
%   comprehensible because its concepts have been introduced in an order that
%   is best for human understanding, using a mixture of formal and informal
%   methods that nicely reinforce each other." [printed "reïnforce"; the
%   preprint lacks "nicely"]
% QUOTE (p. 107, sec. J): "the programmer's task is to state those parts and
%   those relationships, in whatever order is best for human
%   comprehension---not in some rigidly determined order like top-down or
%   bottom-up."
% QUOTE (p. 97, sec. A; Knuth's own caveat): "Programming is a very personal
%   activity, so I can't be certain that what has worked for me will work for
%   everybody."
% VERIFIED: full text, published version (page images on the reMarkable show
%   the journal folios 97 and 107). The deck may call these "the two goals";
%   Knuth does not number them as two goals -- they are the two consecutive
%   paragraphs of sec. A, so "two goals" is the deck's framing.
% COUNTER: not applicable for the definition (known item). Knuth's own
%   caveat (above) and his "ravings of a fanatic" sentence (p. 97) can be
%   shown as the author's own hedge.
% DATE: 2026-11-05
@article{Knuth1984,
  author  = {Knuth, Donald E.},
  title   = {Literate Programming},
  journal = {The Computer Journal},
  volume  = {27},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {97--111},
  year    = {1984},
  doi     = {10.1093/comjnl/27.2.97}
}

% === provenance: SunStaron2026 ===
% CLAIM: Sun and Staron operationalise "LLMs are capable of LP" as embedding
%   probes that must recognise both the programming language and the task of
%   a program; only the embedding models they label trillion-scale
%   (OpenAI text-embedding-3 series, parameter count estimated) reach >70%
%   on both, on Rosetta Code (1,228 tasks, 926 languages) and CodeNet.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search '"literate programming" AND (LLM OR LLMs OR
%   "large language model" OR "large language models" OR "generative AI" OR
%   GenAI OR ChatGPT OR Copilot OR "code generation" OR "coding agent" OR
%   "coding agents" OR "AI-assisted programming" OR "vibe coding")' -p
%   openalex --year 2023- --limit 100 (session "lp-llm-direct", query S1;
%   also returned by S1/ieee, S1/wos, S2, S4, U1). Also in OpenAlex
%   forward chaining from Knuth1984.
% PICKED: the only peer-reviewed journal paper (IEEE TSE) that evaluates
%   LLMs against an explicit LP hypothesis.
% QUOTE (sec. VIII, Conclusions): "Our results indicate that trillion-scale
%   models are the only ones that come close to literate programming by
%   consistently recognizing both the programming language and the programs'
%   corresponding tasks with an accuracy of over 70% on both datasets."
% QUOTE (sec. VII, validity): "it evaluates 'literacy' indirectly through
%   classification accuracy rather than through end-to-end code generation
%   or explanation. Consequently, high probing accuracy may indicate that
%   embeddings capture discriminative features of programming languages and
%   tasks, but not necessarily that models can generate coherent, literate
%   explanations in practice."
% QUOTE (model table footnote): "*:Estimated number, the exact number of
%   parameters is not revealed in public documentation"
% VERIFIED: full text, author's accepted version from research.chalmers.se
%   (549267_Fulltext.pdf), read sections I, II, V, VII-IX and Table V.
%   Table V (SVM): text-embedding-3-large PL 0.8544, PT 0.8703, text 0.9624;
%   LLaMA-3.1-8B PL 0.8552, PT 0.7410, text 0.5338. Status: peer-reviewed,
%   IEEE TSE 52(2):468-487, 2026 (Crossref). Evidence type: computational
%   benchmark (embedding probing); no human participants, no LP documents
%   written or read. Term collision: "literate" here means an LLM's
